WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.704 --> 00:00:14.468
Hi, I'm Mary Chilton, and I am one of the original Pilgrims who survived a year of famine

2
00:00:14.508 --> 00:00:17.348
to celebrate the first Thanksgiving in the New World.

3
00:00:18.889 --> 00:00:24.030
After arriving in the New World, the first thing we needed to do was to write a document

4
00:00:24.490 --> 00:00:26.131
that would guide our new society.

5
00:00:26.871 --> 00:00:30.152
The men gathered together and wrote the Mayflower Compact.

6
00:00:31.184 --> 00:00:37.306
Every man who had traveled aboard the Mayflower signed the agreement on November 21, 1620.

7
00:00:38.326 --> 00:00:43.888
Soon after the Mayflower Compact was signed, the men went exploring to find a place where we could

8
00:00:43.928 --> 00:00:44.988
build our new homes.

9
00:00:46.468 --> 00:00:51.089
After several days they returned to the Mayflower with some corn they had found.

10
00:00:51.109 --> 00:00:56.071
"We think we will find a place to settle soon," they told us hopefully.

11
00:00:56.831 --> 00:00:57.731
We were relieved.

12
00:00:57.751 --> 00:01:01.224
"Oh, thank the Lord," we said.

13
00:01:02.365 --> 00:01:07.648
After six more weeks of exploration, our men sent word that they had found a place to settle.

14
00:01:08.528 --> 00:01:13.791
December 31, 1620, was our first chance to see the place we would call home.

15
00:01:14.532 --> 00:01:15.792
We named it Plymouth.

16
00:01:16.853 --> 00:01:21.956
The weather in January was bad, but the men worked hard to cut logs for the settlement.

17
00:01:23.017 --> 00:01:26.899
First, they built a common house to store our tools, furnishings, and supplies.

18
00:01:27.619 --> 00:01:28.780
Then, they built houses.

19
00:01:29.660 --> 00:01:34.923
The work went slowly because many people were ill, and more than half died in the Great Sickness

20
00:01:34.963 --> 00:01:35.583
that winter.

21
00:01:36.684 --> 00:01:38.645
My family was one of the hardest hit.

22
00:01:39.425 --> 00:01:41.566
I lost both my father and my mother.

23
00:01:42.427 --> 00:01:47.129
My father was the oldest Pilgrim to make the voyage to the New World, and he died very

24
00:01:47.209 --> 00:01:52.172
quickly. My mother was a very strong woman, but she also died.

25
00:01:52.992 --> 00:01:56.254
I know in my heart that she died because she wanted me to live.

26
00:01:57.395 --> 00:01:59.276
She always made sure I ate first.

27
00:02:00.148 --> 00:02:04.349
She would give me some of her food because there wasn't really enough food for all of us

28
00:02:04.409 --> 00:02:04.829
to eat.

29
00:02:05.390 --> 00:02:09.691
By the end of the winter, more than half of those who had crossed on the Mayflower had

30
00:02:09.751 --> 00:02:12.912
died, but the older women were the hardest hit.

31
00:02:14.012 --> 00:02:18.253
Only 5 of the 18 original married women aboard the Mayflower survived.

32
00:02:19.974 --> 00:02:25.435
Although I was now an orphan with no mother or father, the other Pilgrims took me in and

33
00:02:25.516 --> 00:02:26.776
treated me with kindness.

34
00:02:28.964 --> 00:02:32.546
With so many women dead, I found myself busier than ever.

35
00:02:32.566 --> 00:02:37.429
I had to help do the cooking and the laundry for the men and boys who had lost

36
00:02:37.469 --> 00:02:38.630
their wives and mothers.

37
00:02:39.731 --> 00:02:43.873
In March of 1621, a Native American came to our village.

38
00:02:44.634 --> 00:02:46.135
His name was Samoset.

39
00:02:46.815 --> 00:02:49.757
He walked into the village shouting, "Welcome!

40
00:02:50.217 --> 00:02:51.638
Welcome, Englishmen!"

41
00:02:52.658 --> 00:02:57.993
Even though it was a very cold and windy day, Samoset was naked except for a cloth around

42
00:02:58.033 --> 00:02:59.975
his waist and moccasins on his feet.

43
00:03:00.975 --> 00:03:03.256
The men talked to Samoset for a long time.

44
00:03:03.917 --> 00:03:07.098
Samoset left the next day after promising to return soon.

45
00:03:08.119 --> 00:03:15.063
After several days Samoset did return with another Native American who amazed us all with his flawless English.

46
00:03:15.963 --> 00:03:21.506
His name was Squanto, and he said he was the last surviving member of the Patuxet tribe.

47
00:03:22.426 --> 00:03:25.028
Squanto stayed with us that whole spring and summer.

48
00:03:25.824 --> 00:03:28.105
He taught our men how to hunt and how to fish.

49
00:03:28.965 --> 00:03:32.887
He taught the women how to plant and cultivate corn, squash, and beans.

50
00:03:33.927 --> 00:03:36.909
Squanto helped us to make friends with the neighboring tribes.

51
00:03:37.429 --> 00:03:40.930
With his help, we signed a peace treaty with King Massasoit.

52
00:03:41.650 --> 00:03:46.752
We learned that our seeds from England did not grow very well in the New World, but Squanto

53
00:03:46.813 --> 00:03:50.414
showed us that if we planted our seeds with herring, then they would grow.

54
00:03:51.574 --> 00:03:55.116
Squanto and other Native Americans also helped us with our crops.

55
00:03:55.816 --> 00:03:59.779
With their help, our corn, squash, and beans did very well that year.

56
00:04:00.280 --> 00:04:06.505
To celebrate our bountiful harvest, we invited the great King Massasoit and his advisors to our village.

57
00:04:07.225 --> 00:04:09.047
The feast lasted for three days.

58
00:04:09.907 --> 00:04:14.531
We ate duck, turkey, deer, clams, lobster, and many kinds of fish.

59
00:04:15.332 --> 00:04:18.374
There were lots of berries, nuts, and several kinds of fruit.

60
00:04:19.315 --> 00:04:24.399
But the best dish of all was an Indian pudding made from pumpkin and sweetened with the syrup

61
00:04:24.419 --> 00:04:25.280
from a maple tree.

62
00:04:26.332 --> 00:04:31.980
We thanked God for the success of our harvest and for the kindness of the Native Americans who

63
00:04:32.020 --> 00:04:34.443
taught us the skills we needed to survive.
